# Independent audit: obsolete registry row blocks stable generation (#1189)

The strict refusal is correct for its current inputs. Claw c1cbd0f7 unions the
curated primary with every literal canonical exact registry object, then rejects
two namespaces for one permanent subject. Actual invocation/FAILED/stderr and
current source bytes agree. No generator, validator, test, apply or replay was
executed for this audit.

MIM:0002332 is the existing sodium-nitrate 0.70 M stock. Its current identifier,
kg_microbe_node_id, ingredient_type and May 10 curation event explicitly retain
kgmicrobe.ingredient:sodium_nitrate_~28070_m_stock~29 and a broad CHEBI:63005
solute relation. The canonical subject has three rows: that broader ontology
mapping, the matching escaped ingredient identity, and the old B1-generated
kgmicrobe.compound:sodium_nitrate_070_m_stock. This is not an inference from the
parent ontology prefix, numeric ID, label spelling or classifier alone.

The live read of closed Claw #684 describes this exact erroneous extra compound
row. Open #1137 explicitly requires unresolved competing namespaces to fail and
literal valid aliases to survive. MIM #1257 explains why the corrected legacy
generator's additional same-namespace subject handle did not alter this earlier
unselected canonical row. Its legitimate same-namespace alias examples are not
authority to retain an opposing namespace, or to invent the plain ingredient
handle during stable-mode repair.

Recommendation: explicit reviewed retirement is necessary before a successful
generation can omit this row. The generator may emit a diagnostic retirement
proposal supported by these exact bytes, but a proposal must not become an
implicit winner selection. Do not change compatibility_registry_objects to
ignore every canonical object that disagrees with a local primary. Keep the
current failure for all unreviewed conflicts, unknown/ambiguous owners and
numeric registry objects. Preserve legitimate different/mixed-case/escaped
same-namespace aliases exactly.

The least invasive route for this already completed adoption is a separately
reviewed pre-generation canonical retirement using the repository's guarded
publication/receipt primitives. The actual adoption manifest, completed fit
target closure and technical review do not include TC's canonical SSSOM. The
distinct LC canonical remains a pinned legacy input. Thus a narrow TC canonical
correction need not inherently edit or restamp completed adoption. This is a
static boundary finding, not a demonstrated executable correction command or
permission to skip the ordinary completed-adoption verification afterward.

Any such correction must be an explicit immutable operation against the full
current canonical hash and exact old row payload/position, current owner hash,
stable-ID/alias resolution, and independently assessed retirement evidence. It
must archive the complete original canonical bytes and row fields, original
tokens/comment/date/stamp, rationale, review pairs and exact afterimage; preserve
every other physical line; reserve outputs before writing; and refuse changed,
duplicate, unknown, ambiguous or unauthorized targets. It must not add an alias,
new registry identity, ontology edge, role, source binding or release support.
The existing escaped ingredient identity is sufficient for the current stable
subject B1 lookup; no numeric or filename-derived replacement is needed. This
code observation is not a run of B1 or an assertion that every other row passes.

Complete history is a non-negotiable follow-through: merely archiving the removed
row outside the native publication evidence closure is insufficient. The next
stable-publication inspection otherwise sees only the corrected canonical table
and will not automatically include the earlier row among its removed rows.
Before choosing the pre-correction route, establish a supported immutable history
binding that carries the exact original row and correction receipt into final
publication provenance and both current reviews. A genuine separate frozen
historical-review root could use the existing historical_reviews route if its
native source/provenance requirements are actually met; do not fabricate or
restamp a historical report or native publication sidecar. If that binding cannot
be demonstrated, keep the failure and implement an explicit reviewed retirement
input instead of silently dropping history.

An explicit generator exclusion input is a possible longer-term capability,
not an existing flag: it must bind the complete old canonical row and current
owner, genuine review evidence and intended retirement, be captured/rechecked,
appear in the generation manifest and be reproduced by native pure replay.
Current replay admits a fixed complete input set and calls the same four-argument
compatibility function. Adding only a Claw input/skip would not meet that proof;
any coordinated MIM change must also preserve the completed adoption producer
closure rather than editing the already accepted TC code in place.

Current stable-sssom selection cannot solve this beforehand: it requires a
successful generation and accepts REMOVE_WITH_HISTORY only for derived absent
rows. reconcile_sssom repairs ontology/grade/orphan drift, not this local registry
retirement. The inspected Step7a helper supplies guarded transaction primitives
for its two bounded writers, not a ready generic retirement command. This is a
scoped source inspection of those entrypoints, not an exhaustive claim that no
other repository mechanism exists (no gitignore-based absence claim is made).

Required distinguishing regression scope for any chosen implementation: exact
stock retirement with unchanged other lines and full history; genuine unresolved
two-namespace conflict still refused; no invented plain/numeric handle; all valid
same-namespace spellings preserved; wrong/stale/missing/duplicate or unauthorized
retirement evidence refused; changed owner/alias/row evidence refused; guarded
write/recovery/receipt-currentness retained; native replay includes any new input.
Reuse existing passing tests; execute only new or affected checks plus required
native validation once an independently reviewed implementation exists.

Scientific limits: this establishes the documented generated-row defect and
supports a narrowly scoped retirement proposal. It does not newly verify the
stock's complete recipe, solvent, quantities, solute synonyms or chemical-property
placement, and it does not approve the remaining complete row for consumers.
The existing source and scientific holds, including unrelated #1367, remain.
No records, code, canonical bytes, guards, policies, completed receipts or Git
state were modified. Root owns issue #1189, implementation and any future action.
